WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.570 --> 00:00:04.920
Welcome to Ear Biscuits,

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:07.070
the podcast where two lifelong friends

00:00:07.070 --> 00:00:09.135
talk about life for a long time.

00:00:09.135 --> 00:00:09.968
I'm Rhett.

00:00:09.968 --> 00:00:11.740
With Matthew McConaughey sometimes.

00:00:11.740 --> 00:00:13.720
Oh no, you're not Matthew McConaughey?

00:00:13.720 --> 00:00:15.880
Yeah, we don't have
him here for that part.

00:00:15.880 --> 00:00:17.010
And I'm Link.

00:00:17.010 --> 00:00:18.700
This week at the round
table of dim lighting,

00:00:18.700 --> 00:00:20.937
We are talking to Matthew McConaughey.

00:00:20.937 --> 00:00:22.310
Expanding the circle.

00:00:22.310 --> 00:00:23.803
It is not an interview.

00:00:24.830 --> 00:00:26.610
We wanted it to be a conversation.

00:00:26.610 --> 00:00:27.860
We wanted it to be an extension

00:00:27.860 --> 00:00:29.830
of what Ear Biscuits has become,

00:00:29.830 --> 00:00:32.630
even though it is exceptional

00:00:32.630 --> 00:00:34.300
that we decided to do an interview.

00:00:34.300 --> 00:00:36.110
And we didn't do it just because,

00:00:36.110 --> 00:00:38.940
well, he's so famous,
we gotta talk to him.

00:00:38.940 --> 00:00:42.280
It was that. And it was more than that.

00:00:42.280 --> 00:00:44.719
It was personal for us, honestly.

00:00:44.719 --> 00:00:47.540
We're not about to make Ear Biscuits

00:00:47.540 --> 00:00:49.140
anything other than what it is.

00:00:49.140 --> 00:00:50.470
Right.

00:00:50.470 --> 00:00:52.460
For for the most part,

00:00:52.460 --> 00:00:53.950
we kind of broke the seal on, okay,

00:00:53.950 --> 00:00:55.330
we can bring in a guest sometimes.

00:00:55.330 --> 00:00:58.160
But the reason that we decided to do this

00:00:58.160 --> 00:01:00.840
is it goes back to,

00:01:00.840 --> 00:01:03.850
I guess it was beginning of 2020,

00:01:03.850 --> 00:01:04.960
is when we were actually,

00:01:04.960 --> 00:01:08.970
we were given Matthew's book Greenlights,

00:01:08.970 --> 00:01:11.060
which is something that
happens pretty often, right.

00:01:11.060 --> 00:01:13.900
Celebrities write books
and then their team

00:01:13.900 --> 00:01:15.940
send them out to other celebrities

00:01:15.940 --> 00:01:17.360
or people who have an audience.

00:01:17.360 --> 00:01:19.650
So they might read them
and talk about them.

00:01:19.650 --> 00:01:21.760
so we ended up getting quite a few books

00:01:24.214 --> 00:01:26.410
and I saw this, his book come in

00:01:26.410 --> 00:01:27.783
and I was like, oh, this is interesting.

00:01:27.783 --> 00:01:31.743
This is more of like a
memoir kind of thing.

00:01:32.780 --> 00:01:34.340
But I didn't immediately like say, okay,

00:01:34.340 --> 00:01:35.580
I'm gonna start reading this thing.

00:01:35.580 --> 00:01:38.210
It was my good friend's Lance and Lacey,

00:01:38.210 --> 00:01:39.724
of Beard and Lady,

00:01:39.724 --> 00:01:41.870
who make all the grooming
products from Mythical.

00:01:41.870 --> 00:01:42.703
Oh, really?

00:01:42.703 --> 00:01:45.450
They live a life of adventure.

00:01:45.450 --> 00:01:50.290
And they live a life
of embracing new ideas

00:01:50.290 --> 00:01:51.670
and new challenges.

00:01:51.670 --> 00:01:56.440
And following those things, for instance.

00:01:56.440 --> 00:01:57.560
With gusto.

00:01:57.560 --> 00:02:02.280
Deciding to revitalize
a historic haunted hotel

00:02:02.280 --> 00:02:07.280
in Arkansas and turn it into
a hotel that you can visit.

00:02:07.450 --> 00:02:09.490
That's like the latest
thing that they've done.

00:02:09.490 --> 00:02:11.440
They're always doing something like that.

00:02:11.440 --> 00:02:14.147
We wouldn't have our grooming products

00:02:14.147 --> 00:02:15.990
if it wasn't for them hatching that idea.

00:02:15.990 --> 00:02:16.823
Yeah.

00:02:16.823 --> 00:02:18.050
And so when they said,

00:02:18.050 --> 00:02:19.650
have you listened to Greenlights yet?

00:02:19.650 --> 00:02:22.660
And Jesse and I have a
text thread with them.

00:02:22.660 --> 00:02:26.850
That is very much that have
you watched this thing?

00:02:26.850 --> 00:02:28.950
I told you when we went home for Christmas

00:02:29.950 --> 00:02:32.158
or whenever it was, for the summer,

00:02:32.158 --> 00:02:34.166
they were like you got to watch this.

00:02:34.166 --> 00:02:35.968
Yeah, they knew the stuff to watch.

00:02:35.968 --> 00:02:37.726
I watched Bo Burnham: Insides with them.

00:02:37.726 --> 00:02:39.100
Like I waited specifically
to watch that with them.

00:02:39.100 --> 00:02:39.933
But anyway.

00:02:39.933 --> 00:02:41.306
So, the recommendation coming from them.

00:02:41.306 --> 00:02:42.139
It was like, all right, I'll do it.

00:02:42.139 --> 00:02:42.972
Yeah and so.

00:02:42.972 --> 00:02:44.092
Did you read or listen to it?

00:02:44.092 --> 00:02:45.420
I listened to it because
I've kind of moved

00:02:45.420 --> 00:02:47.673
almost exclusively into audio books?

00:02:49.107 --> 00:02:50.910
There's not a small amount of shame

00:02:50.910 --> 00:02:52.220
that comes with that,

00:02:52.220 --> 00:02:53.053
just for me personally,

00:02:53.053 --> 00:02:55.560
because I have this bias towards reading.

00:02:55.560 --> 00:02:57.970
But I can do anything
else while I'm listening.

00:02:57.970 --> 00:03:00.110
So I just ended up listening
to all kinds of books,

00:03:00.110 --> 00:03:03.160
like multiple books a month
is like my pace now, right?

00:03:03.160 --> 00:03:04.102
So.

00:03:04.102 --> 00:03:07.760
And then you started talking
to me about aspects of it,

00:03:07.760 --> 00:03:12.760
like just how his approach
to being, expressing himself

00:03:13.060 --> 00:03:15.410
and being creative and
going after his muse,

00:03:15.410 --> 00:03:17.182
Well, in 90% percent of it.

00:03:17.182 --> 00:03:18.015
And the principles of it.

00:03:18.015 --> 00:03:22.390
Well, 90% to begin with
was, man, you can't believe

00:03:22.390 --> 00:03:24.090
this thing that happened
to Matthew McConaughey.

00:03:24.090 --> 00:03:25.367
Right, right. Just a great story.

00:03:25.367 --> 00:03:27.850
And you can't believe the
way that Matthew McConaughey

00:03:27.850 --> 00:03:30.140
decides to tell you the story.

00:03:30.140 --> 00:03:32.350
Everybody knows Matthew
McConaughey at this point.

00:03:32.350 --> 00:03:34.650
So you can imagine how he's
going to tell this story.

00:03:34.650 --> 00:03:38.310
But so listening to him do it
in his very McConaughey way

00:03:38.310 --> 00:03:39.930
is just, it was a treat.

00:03:39.930 --> 00:03:42.987
And I was resisting telling you the story,

00:03:42.987 --> 00:03:44.160
you know how when you get into something,

00:03:44.160 --> 00:03:46.270
you want to just tell people the stories.

00:03:46.270 --> 00:03:50.140
And you're like Mr. Spoiler
reverse to the nth degree.

00:03:50.140 --> 00:03:50.980
So I had to be like, well,

00:03:50.980 --> 00:03:53.530
there's a story about
him going to Australia

00:03:53.530 --> 00:03:55.727
and it's like, okay,
well, I've been Australia.

00:03:55.727 --> 00:03:57.740
You can't give away too much,

00:03:57.740 --> 00:04:00.750
but then you ended up getting into it.

00:04:00.750 --> 00:04:02.810
He went as a foreign exchange student,

00:04:02.810 --> 00:04:04.167
you should say that.

00:04:04.167 --> 00:04:05.000
Yeah.

00:04:05.000 --> 00:04:07.010
I didn't listen to it
for a number of weeks

00:04:07.010 --> 00:04:07.960
because I had other stuff.

00:04:07.960 --> 00:04:09.640
But then I had hit this dry spell

00:04:09.640 --> 00:04:11.410
and I was like, I need something.

00:04:11.410 --> 00:04:14.890
And I started listening
to it two weeks ago

00:04:16.970 --> 00:04:19.640
and then after I was almost
done with it is when,

00:04:19.640 --> 00:04:21.450
they were like, hey, he
wants to come on your show.

00:04:21.450 --> 00:04:24.240
We're like, I think we
gotta make this happen

00:04:24.240 --> 00:04:27.087
because it resonated with us so much.

00:04:27.087 --> 00:04:27.920
Yeah.

00:04:27.920 --> 00:04:31.520
So as you'll see, we turned it into us,

00:04:31.520 --> 00:04:35.640
getting what we needed out
of it and our own purposes.

00:04:35.640 --> 00:04:38.490
It's not really an interview, as you can.

00:04:38.490 --> 00:04:42.020
I would advise most interviewers
to not begin interviews

00:04:42.020 --> 00:04:44.730
by just telling stories about
themselves, like we did,

00:04:44.730 --> 00:04:47.180
it was kind of like what
Chris Farley used to do on SNL

00:04:47.180 --> 00:04:48.013
when he was.

00:04:48.013 --> 00:04:49.213
Remember that time when.

00:04:50.142 --> 00:04:50.975
No, we didn't do that.

00:04:50.975 --> 00:04:53.450
No, but I had a feeling,

00:04:53.450 --> 00:04:56.560
not knowing Matthew
personally, but knowing of him

00:04:56.560 --> 00:04:59.780
and in listening to the
book and I had a feeling

00:04:59.780 --> 00:05:01.700
that he would just get right into the.

00:05:01.700 --> 00:05:02.533
Yeah.

00:05:02.533 --> 00:05:06.223
He's a let's have a campfire
conversation kind of guy.

00:05:07.190 --> 00:05:09.530
And so he just, he stepped right in here

00:05:09.530 --> 00:05:11.070
and it would have been nice

00:05:11.070 --> 00:05:13.520
to have an actually at the table,

00:05:13.520 --> 00:05:14.780
but you know what.

00:05:14.780 --> 00:05:15.820
He's still on video chat,

00:05:15.820 --> 00:05:16.772
It is what it is.

00:05:16.772 --> 00:05:18.110
So if you watch the Video
version, it's there.

00:05:18.110 --> 00:05:19.120
All right, let's get to it.

00:05:19.120 --> 00:05:21.420
But first we always want to encourage you

00:05:21.420 --> 00:05:24.160
to go to a mythical.com to rep your boys.

00:05:24.160 --> 00:05:26.440
Do you have any clue what's over there?

00:05:26.440 --> 00:05:27.690
If you haven't been over there,

00:05:27.690 --> 00:05:28.872
There's a lot of stuff.

00:05:28.872 --> 00:05:29.705
In a couple of weeks,

00:05:29.705 --> 00:05:30.563
it's gonna be new stuff.

00:05:31.442 --> 00:05:33.460
So go to mythical.com, browse it.

00:05:33.460 --> 00:05:35.010
You don't have to make any commitments.

00:05:35.010 --> 00:05:37.080
And if you find yourself
purchasing something,

00:05:37.080 --> 00:05:38.290
then thank you.

00:05:38.290 --> 00:05:40.580
And if you want something,

00:05:40.580 --> 00:05:42.000
things come and things go.

00:05:42.000 --> 00:05:43.840
There's actually the last chance section,

00:05:43.840 --> 00:05:46.680
where things that will go
away and never come back.

00:05:46.680 --> 00:05:47.840
We've put that all together

00:05:47.840 --> 00:05:49.660
in one nice little section for you.

00:05:49.660 --> 00:05:50.493
Grab it.

00:05:50.493 --> 00:05:51.520
So you won't complain about not being able

00:05:51.520 --> 00:05:54.420
to get something in the last
chance section, mythical.com.

00:05:59.210 --> 00:06:00.430
It's great to meet you.

00:06:00.430 --> 00:06:02.610
We don't typically have
guests on our podcast,

00:06:02.610 --> 00:06:05.550
but we both read and
listened to Greenlights

00:06:05.550 --> 00:06:07.790
and we love good stories.

00:06:07.790 --> 00:06:10.583
And you got some good stories, man.

00:06:12.673 --> 00:06:13.660
And we loved it.

00:06:13.660 --> 00:06:15.030
It's been many moons

00:06:15.030 --> 00:06:16.900
since we have had a conversation

00:06:16.900 --> 00:06:17.813
outside of just the two of us talking,

00:06:17.813 --> 00:06:20.000
because we figured out
we've got quite a bit

00:06:20.000 --> 00:06:21.740
that we can just talk
about to each other about.

00:06:21.740 --> 00:06:23.380
Yeah.

00:06:23.380 --> 00:06:27.360
But we both connected with your book

00:06:27.360 --> 00:06:30.250
and I mean, lots of people
have connected with your book

00:06:30.250 --> 00:06:35.250
and in a big way, but something
about your stage of life,

00:06:36.630 --> 00:06:40.570
your story kind of coming
from small town South.

00:06:40.570 --> 00:06:42.633
There was a lot that we could relate to.

00:06:43.680 --> 00:06:45.190
And there's a lot that's on our minds

00:06:45.190 --> 00:06:47.410
that I think selfishly,

00:06:47.410 --> 00:06:51.210
when you start talking
about legacy in Greenlights.

00:06:51.210 --> 00:06:52.280
Yeah.

00:06:52.280 --> 00:06:55.970
That's like we're having so
many conversations about that

00:06:55.970 --> 00:06:57.200
and also on this podcast.

00:06:57.200 --> 00:06:58.280
So I think selfishly,

00:06:58.280 --> 00:07:00.130
we just want to pick
your brain a little bit

00:07:00.130 --> 00:07:02.020
and kind of unpack some more of that.

00:07:02.020 --> 00:07:03.666
But first, when it comes to these stories.

00:07:03.666 --> 00:07:04.499
Right.

00:07:05.360 --> 00:07:07.080
And just relating to that.

00:07:07.080 --> 00:07:11.500
There's a couple of
uncanny, uncanny parallels.

00:07:11.500 --> 00:07:13.650
I love you're talking about your mom,

00:07:13.650 --> 00:07:18.259
basically telling you to
plagiarize the poem that then.

00:07:19.860 --> 00:07:20.693
I won.

00:07:20.693 --> 00:07:22.260
And then you won.

00:07:22.260 --> 00:07:24.760
I won whatever, the seventh
grade poetry contest

00:07:24.760 --> 00:07:27.233
Yeah. Because I mean talk about, yeah,

00:07:29.170 --> 00:07:32.450
because does the poem
mean something to you?

00:07:33.488 --> 00:07:34.321
Yeah, I understand it.

00:07:34.321 --> 00:07:35.350
So you like it?

00:07:35.350 --> 00:07:39.140
Yeah. It brings up a feeling in you Matt.

00:07:39.140 --> 00:07:41.250
Yeah, it says if all
that I would want to do

00:07:41.250 --> 00:07:43.030
would be to sit and talk
to you, would you listen?

00:07:43.030 --> 00:07:45.810
That's like, yeah mom.

00:07:45.810 --> 00:07:46.930
So you understand that?

00:07:46.930 --> 00:07:49.020
Yeah, then write that.

00:07:49.020 --> 00:07:51.550
Well, it's Anne Ashberry

00:07:52.530 --> 00:07:53.520
No, but as I said,

00:07:53.520 --> 00:07:55.770
you sign your name
because you understand it.

00:07:58.530 --> 00:07:59.363
That was her reason.

00:07:59.363 --> 00:08:00.900
It means something to you, it's yours.

00:08:00.900 --> 00:08:03.090
Now, here's my version of my mom.

00:08:03.090 --> 00:08:07.230
We had this poster contest.

00:08:07.230 --> 00:08:08.140
County-wide.

00:08:08.140 --> 00:08:13.050
County-wide, Harnett
County, anti-drug message.

00:08:13.050 --> 00:08:14.010
And I told my mom,

00:08:14.010 --> 00:08:17.120
now my mom worked at the
public health department

00:08:17.120 --> 00:08:20.123
and there was a pamphlet for everything.

00:08:21.200 --> 00:08:22.290
And so she would come,

00:08:22.290 --> 00:08:24.840
that's how I learned about
sex, through a pamphlet.

00:08:26.776 --> 00:08:27.609
She brought home a pamphlet.

00:08:27.609 --> 00:08:29.530
She said, well, Link,
there's an anti-drug one

00:08:29.530 --> 00:08:31.110
and look, there's a picture right here.

00:08:31.110 --> 00:08:32.300
And it says,

00:08:32.300 --> 00:08:35.490
there are lots of fun things
to do instead of drugs.

00:08:35.490 --> 00:08:37.600
And then it's a guy holding
all these other things,

00:08:37.600 --> 00:08:40.653
like badminton thing.

00:08:41.674 --> 00:08:42.507
Yeah.

00:08:42.507 --> 00:08:45.108
And like an art art easel
and stuff like that.

00:08:45.108 --> 00:08:47.587
And so she basically said, draw that.

00:08:49.160 --> 00:08:50.860
But here's the thing about my mom,

00:08:52.130 --> 00:08:54.290
I don't even think she
knew what plagiarism was.

00:08:54.290 --> 00:08:57.790
Yeah, she didn't. There was no process.

00:08:57.790 --> 00:08:59.340
Well, she explained it to make it okay.

00:08:59.340 --> 00:09:01.330
She didn't have a pithy tenet

00:09:01.330 --> 00:09:05.440
that you can prescribe in your book.

00:09:05.440 --> 00:09:06.754
For her, it was just very innocent.

00:09:06.754 --> 00:09:07.587
Right, right, right.

00:09:07.587 --> 00:09:08.420
This is a great idea,

00:09:08.420 --> 00:09:10.228
Why don't you just do exactly that?

00:09:10.228 --> 00:09:11.061
Right, right, right.

00:09:11.061 --> 00:09:12.760
And I did it and I won.

00:09:12.760 --> 00:09:13.593
By a long shot,

00:09:13.593 --> 00:09:14.426
And you won. Okay

00:09:14.426 --> 00:09:15.351
I won baby.

00:09:15.351 --> 00:09:16.184
Here you go.

00:09:16.184 --> 00:09:17.017
He won the whole county, which.

00:09:17.017 --> 00:09:17.850
I believed it.

00:09:17.850 --> 00:09:20.580
And listen, as his best
friend at the time.

00:09:20.580 --> 00:09:22.206
What grade was that?

00:09:22.206 --> 00:09:23.733
Fourth or fifth?

00:09:25.170 --> 00:09:26.550
I had no idea.

00:09:26.550 --> 00:09:27.383
I didn't tell anybody.

00:09:27.383 --> 00:09:30.130
I thought you had done dug really deep.

00:09:30.130 --> 00:09:30.963
Yeah.

00:09:30.963 --> 00:09:31.796
Deeper than ever.

00:09:32.740 --> 00:09:33.630
I still have it.

00:09:33.630 --> 00:09:37.210
I have a laminated poster
of this in my office

00:09:37.210 --> 00:09:38.350
that somebody mailed me.

00:09:38.350 --> 00:09:39.480
With a blue ribbon on it.

00:09:39.480 --> 00:09:40.313
Yeah.

00:09:40.313 --> 00:09:41.146
And now the other thing

00:09:41.146 --> 00:09:43.280
that was a bit of an uncanny parallel.

00:09:43.280 --> 00:09:48.280
So my dad is a bit of a
larger than life character

00:09:48.680 --> 00:09:51.000
at any time throughout my life.

00:09:51.000 --> 00:09:52.890
Even when I felt like I
was afraid of nothing,

00:09:52.890 --> 00:09:54.640
I've always been afraid of him, right?

00:09:54.640 --> 00:09:55.693
Right. Yeah.

00:09:58.129 --> 00:10:00.423
And in a good way, the good fear.

00:10:01.789 --> 00:10:06.610
And so by the time that we
were seniors in high school,

00:10:08.640 --> 00:10:10.320
in Buies Creek, North Carolina,

00:10:10.320 --> 00:10:12.750
where nobody that we knew

00:10:12.750 --> 00:10:14.730
had taken any sort of entertainment path.

00:10:14.730 --> 00:10:16.120
Our idea of being an entertainer

00:10:16.120 --> 00:10:18.580
was the guy who would come
to the middle school dances

00:10:18.580 --> 00:10:21.593
and do, he was either a DJ
or he could be a magician.

00:10:23.320 --> 00:10:24.180
That was the guy.

00:10:24.180 --> 00:10:25.780
I've seen him do both at once.

00:10:27.842 --> 00:10:28.770
And so, but we had this idea,

00:10:28.770 --> 00:10:30.220
we want to go to film school.

00:10:31.470 --> 00:10:34.480
And I had that conversation with my dad

00:10:35.410 --> 00:10:36.970
that you had with your dad.

00:10:36.970 --> 00:10:38.340
When you called him up and said,

00:10:38.340 --> 00:10:39.720
I'm thinking about going to film school.

00:10:39.720 --> 00:10:41.670
And that was pretty built up for you.

00:10:44.001 --> 00:10:44.834
Yeah.

00:10:44.834 --> 00:10:46.690
You kind of had to like breathe
into that moment, right?

00:10:46.690 --> 00:10:47.743
Very much so.

00:10:49.190 --> 00:10:52.300
It was a moment where
I wasn't sleeping well

00:10:52.300 --> 00:10:55.260
at the idea of going to law school.

00:10:55.260 --> 00:10:57.250
So that was keeping me up at night.

00:10:57.250 --> 00:11:00.640
Then it was weeks of
sharing stories and stuff.

00:11:00.640 --> 00:11:03.210
I'd written with my buddy,
Rob Bindler, who was NYU,

00:11:03.210 --> 00:11:07.040
who was the only film guy I knew at all.

00:11:07.040 --> 00:11:08.993
And plus he was at Longview, Texas.

00:11:10.300 --> 00:11:12.120
He didn't watch many films there either.

00:11:12.120 --> 00:11:14.040
So I'm sending him stuff in New York.

00:11:14.040 --> 00:11:14.873
He's writing back, going,

00:11:14.873 --> 00:11:15.897
man, this is pretty good, this is good.

00:11:15.897 --> 00:11:18.380
And I knew he'd give me an honest opinion.

00:11:18.380 --> 00:11:19.760
And then I get the confidence.

00:11:19.760 --> 00:11:21.540
Then I find the book, The
Greatest Salesman In The World,

00:11:21.540 --> 00:11:23.050
the Og Mandino book.

00:11:23.050 --> 00:11:24.800
Sometimes a piece of art or a book

00:11:24.800 --> 00:11:27.220
will find you or a person
will share something with you.

00:11:27.220 --> 00:11:30.908
And you're like, they've
been reading my mail.

00:11:30.908 --> 00:11:31.741
Yeah.

00:11:31.741 --> 00:11:32.574
This is direct line.

00:11:32.574 --> 00:11:34.103
This was written for me right now,

00:11:34.103 --> 00:11:35.490
I have no question about it,

00:11:35.490 --> 00:11:36.860
when we have that clarity,

00:11:36.860 --> 00:11:39.190
whether it's true or
not, it is true, right?

00:11:39.190 --> 00:11:40.023
Yeah.

00:11:40.023 --> 00:11:41.123
So that book found me,

00:11:41.123 --> 00:11:42.750
gave me the courage to say,

00:11:42.750 --> 00:11:43.890
that's what I'm doing.

00:11:43.890 --> 00:11:44.830
And then I had to plan out

00:11:44.830 --> 00:11:46.340
when the best time to call dad was

00:11:46.340 --> 00:11:47.390
since he was paying tuition.

00:11:47.390 --> 00:11:48.450
So I say, like I said in the book,

00:11:48.450 --> 00:11:50.870
I picked that Tuesday
night, seven o'clock,

00:11:50.870 --> 00:11:51.890
he's home from work.

00:11:51.890 --> 00:11:53.020
He's already eaten dinner,

00:11:53.020 --> 00:11:54.920
he's having a beer with mom on the couch,

00:11:54.920 --> 00:11:56.039
best time to get it.

00:11:56.039 --> 00:11:57.057
Yeah.

00:11:57.057 --> 00:11:57.890
Not Monday, Tuesday.

00:11:57.890 --> 00:12:01.243
And I called him 19:36
pm and laid it on him.

00:12:02.831 --> 00:12:04.570
And he gave me that great response.

00:12:04.570 --> 00:12:07.560
Okay, more that yes, don't half ass it,

00:12:07.560 --> 00:12:10.160
but go on about your story about.

00:12:10.160 --> 00:12:11.166
Well.

00:12:11.166 --> 00:12:11.999
You coming out of North Carolina.

00:12:11.999 --> 00:12:13.800
The thing is that I must have not,

00:12:13.800 --> 00:12:15.680
I did not plan as well as you did,

00:12:15.680 --> 00:12:17.250
because when I told my dad,

00:12:17.250 --> 00:12:19.640
dad, I'm thinking about going,

00:12:19.640 --> 00:12:22.188
Link and I are thinking
about going to film school.

00:12:23.160 --> 00:12:23.993
Right.

00:12:23.993 --> 00:12:26.780
It had been years since
we had done the blood oath

00:12:26.780 --> 00:12:28.900
and said, we're gonna
create something together.

00:12:28.900 --> 00:12:30.550
And like, we had really colluded

00:12:30.550 --> 00:12:31.669
and we had each other.

00:12:31.669 --> 00:12:32.502
Right.

00:12:32.502 --> 00:12:35.370
And we like, worked up
each other's courage.

00:12:35.370 --> 00:12:36.710
Well, we had this plan.

00:12:36.710 --> 00:12:38.760
At one point, it was okay,

00:12:38.760 --> 00:12:41.180
we're going to go to UNC Asheville.

00:12:41.180 --> 00:12:42.750
I'm gonna play basketball,

00:12:42.750 --> 00:12:44.670
but they have a film program

00:12:44.670 --> 00:12:45.503
and you're gonna.

00:12:45.503 --> 00:12:48.453
I'm just gonna keep score. I've done that.

00:12:48.453 --> 00:12:50.150
That didn't really come to fruition.

00:12:50.150 --> 00:12:51.170
But then it was just like,

00:12:51.170 --> 00:12:52.960
okay, are you gonna do what
everybody else is doing

00:12:52.960 --> 00:12:55.760
and kind of just go on to
the state school or whatever.

00:12:55.760 --> 00:12:58.580
And I said, dad, I want
to go to film school.

00:12:58.580 --> 00:13:00.170
He didn't say, don't half ass it, he said,

00:13:00.170 --> 00:13:01.478
well, I'm not paying for that.

00:13:02.827 --> 00:13:03.660
Heard. Yeah.

00:13:03.660 --> 00:13:05.060
But the funny thing is

00:13:05.060 --> 00:13:07.780
and we put that in our book,

00:13:07.780 --> 00:13:09.460
the Book of Mythicality
we wrote a few years ago,

00:13:09.460 --> 00:13:13.123
by the way, Matthew Inman, also
our editor, incredible guy.

00:13:14.270 --> 00:13:16.400
Oh yes. Matthew, Matthew E?

00:13:16.400 --> 00:13:17.233
Yeah.

00:13:17.233 --> 00:13:18.407
Oh, Inman, Inman?

00:13:18.407 --> 00:13:19.650
Inman, yeah.

00:13:19.650 --> 00:13:22.379
Inman, great and what great hair.

00:13:22.379 --> 00:13:24.623
I cannot quit complimenting
him on his hair.

00:13:25.769 --> 00:13:27.491
Oh my God, he's got the greatest hair.

00:13:27.491 --> 00:13:28.347
And we actually,

00:13:28.347 --> 00:13:30.219
we experienced the transition of his hair.

00:13:30.219 --> 00:13:31.052
That's true.

00:13:31.052 --> 00:13:32.060
Because in book one, he.

00:13:32.060 --> 00:13:33.330
I liked to think we inspired it.

00:13:33.330 --> 00:13:35.910
It was, it was close
cropped, but book two,

00:13:35.910 --> 00:13:38.023
it was a new whole new thing.

00:13:40.020 --> 00:13:42.240
Because I put that in
the book and my dad said,

00:13:42.240 --> 00:13:44.330
you know, that's not what I said,

00:13:44.330 --> 00:13:47.000
you remember that incorrectly.

00:13:47.000 --> 00:13:48.534
He was like, I made for a better story.

00:13:48.534 --> 00:13:49.530
Right, what did he say?

00:13:49.530 --> 00:13:51.690
He said, well, I must
have said something that,

00:13:51.690 --> 00:13:53.410
that was what you felt.

00:13:53.410 --> 00:13:55.210
Yeah.

00:13:55.210 --> 00:13:56.180
And that was the result.

00:13:56.180 --> 00:13:58.433
Hey, he and my mom may know each other.

00:13:59.652 --> 00:14:00.728
Yeah.

00:14:00.728 --> 00:14:01.935
Yeah. I could see into it

00:14:01.935 --> 00:14:04.200
that he was not very
supportive of that idea.

00:14:04.200 --> 00:14:05.480
So we went to engineering school

00:14:05.480 --> 00:14:06.742
and the rest is history.

00:14:06.742 --> 00:14:07.575
Yeah.

00:14:07.575 --> 00:14:08.408
And it kind of worked itself out,

00:14:08.408 --> 00:14:09.350
we took a more circuitous path.

00:14:09.350 --> 00:14:12.360
So, you'll you both went together.

00:14:12.360 --> 00:14:13.193
Yeah.

00:14:13.193 --> 00:14:14.870
Off the same school, engineering school.

00:14:14.870 --> 00:14:16.930
And you end back up here
sitting next to each other,

00:14:16.930 --> 00:14:19.860
having your platform, a
form of entertainment.

00:14:19.860 --> 00:14:20.906
Yeah.

00:14:20.906 --> 00:14:22.370
Film storytelling here,
in front of the mic.

00:14:22.370 --> 00:14:23.203
Yeah.

00:14:23.203 --> 00:14:24.036
So it ended up.

00:14:24.036 --> 00:14:25.425
Yeah, we found a way.

00:14:25.425 --> 00:14:26.258
Who knows what would have happened.

00:14:26.258 --> 00:14:28.940
We found a way, it was
quite a circuitous path,

00:14:28.940 --> 00:14:30.130
but I think it turned out

00:14:30.130 --> 00:14:32.640
to be the most strategic way for us

00:14:32.640 --> 00:14:33.810
to get to where we wanted to get.

00:14:33.810 --> 00:14:35.346
We just didn't know that.

00:14:35.346 --> 00:14:36.179
I hear you.

00:14:36.179 --> 00:14:37.670
So he did us a favor.

00:14:37.670 --> 00:14:40.540
Yeah. it's all science looking back.

00:14:40.540 --> 00:14:41.516
Yeah.

00:14:41.516 --> 00:14:42.532
And history going forward,

00:14:42.532 --> 00:14:43.460
but you can connect all the dots,

00:14:43.460 --> 00:14:45.880
whatever your dad said, or didn't say,

00:14:45.880 --> 00:14:48.960
whatever you felt was
that ain't happening.

00:14:48.960 --> 00:14:50.453
So derail and I'm going my own way

00:14:50.453 --> 00:14:52.310
and you end up right back and you go,

00:14:52.310 --> 00:14:54.200
damn what if he would've said
yeah, I go to film school.

00:14:54.200 --> 00:14:55.650
Probably wouldn't work out.

00:14:56.887 --> 00:14:57.937
Exactly. Yeah.

00:14:57.937 --> 00:15:01.020
Well, one of the things that
we really connected with

00:15:01.020 --> 00:15:03.560
and something that's kind
of been on our minds,

00:15:03.560 --> 00:15:08.560
relates to your sort of your
very intentional decision

00:15:08.570 --> 00:15:10.967
that you made, to say,

00:15:10.967 --> 00:15:13.639
I'm not gonna work on
another romcom, right?

00:15:13.639 --> 00:15:14.472
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:15:14.472 --> 00:15:15.305
So talk a little,

00:15:15.305 --> 00:15:18.160
just for anybody who isn't
familiar with that decision,

00:15:18.160 --> 00:15:19.859
kind of gives them
context for that decision.

00:15:19.859 --> 00:15:20.692
Yeah.

00:15:20.692 --> 00:15:23.030
And then as it relates to your your legacy

00:15:23.030 --> 00:15:24.030
is kind of where we want to take it.

00:15:24.030 --> 00:15:25.063
Sure, sure.

00:15:26.170 --> 00:15:30.360
I think it's around 2010ish
or so maybe a little earlier,

00:15:30.360 --> 00:15:32.150
but I'm rolling in romcoms.

00:15:32.150 --> 00:15:35.570
I've just done had like four in a row

00:15:35.570 --> 00:15:37.350
that have all done well.

00:15:37.350 --> 00:15:40.550
Their romcoms are middle
budget, studio pictures.

00:15:40.550 --> 00:15:43.513
Meaning at that time is
around $35 million budgets.

00:15:44.730 --> 00:15:46.220
It's above the line cost

00:15:46.220 --> 00:15:48.080
because you don't have
a huge production value.

00:15:48.080 --> 00:15:50.150
It's not, you're not like
making a Chris Nolan film,

00:15:50.150 --> 00:15:52.700
where you're spending all
this money on production.

00:15:53.957 --> 00:15:57.390
And it's about how the
two, in most romcoms,

00:15:57.390 --> 00:15:58.480
how the guy and the girl get along.

00:15:58.480 --> 00:16:00.140
It's what the chemistry of them is.

00:16:00.140 --> 00:16:03.330
And we know they're going
to meet me at the beginning,

00:16:03.330 --> 00:16:04.840
break up for some reason in the middle

00:16:04.840 --> 00:16:06.240
and then he'll chase her at the end

00:16:06.240 --> 00:16:07.937
and catch her on a bridge
or a moped or something.

00:16:07.937 --> 00:16:08.930
And the credits will roll.

00:16:08.930 --> 00:16:10.150
We'll be happy, we got there.

00:16:10.150 --> 00:16:11.690
Then the fairytale is good.

00:16:11.690 --> 00:16:12.523
We've seen it all.

00:16:12.523 --> 00:16:13.356
We know it's going to end,

00:16:13.356 --> 00:16:15.578
but we just want to see it again

00:16:15.578 --> 00:16:17.820
because who are the leads usually in it?

00:16:17.820 --> 00:16:19.970
Do we want to follow
them in this love story?

00:16:19.970 --> 00:16:20.959
Yeah.

00:16:20.959 --> 00:16:22.160
So I've done four of these there.

00:16:22.160 --> 00:16:23.670
I'm the romcom guy in Hollywood.

00:16:23.670 --> 00:16:25.370
Man, I took the baton from Hugh Grant

00:16:25.370 --> 00:16:26.350
and I'm running with it.

00:16:26.350 --> 00:16:27.970
That's my lane.

00:16:27.970 --> 00:16:30.239
I've got ownership of that lane.

00:16:30.239 --> 00:16:32.930
I'm the go-to guy. I like doing them.

00:16:32.930 --> 00:16:35.360
They're fun. I call them
Saturday afternoon characters.

00:16:35.360 --> 00:16:37.960
So like a flip-flop character.
You don't take romcom.

00:16:37.960 --> 00:16:39.394
You don't get in there

00:16:39.394 --> 00:16:41.900
and hang your hat on humanity.

00:16:41.900 --> 00:16:44.310
No, you're supposed to
bounce from cloud to cloud

00:16:44.310 --> 00:16:45.143
and keep them light.

00:16:45.143 --> 00:16:46.790
And I believe I did a decent job of that.

00:16:46.790 --> 00:16:48.373
Anyway with their success,

00:16:49.340 --> 00:16:52.980
they became so successful that
the dramas I did want to do

00:16:52.980 --> 00:16:54.990
were I did want to hang my hat on humanity

00:16:54.990 --> 00:16:57.713
and see if I could really
flex my active muscles.

00:16:58.664 --> 00:17:00.567
The studio system saying no
way McConaughey. no, thank you.

00:17:00.567 --> 00:17:03.470
And I say, wait, I'll take a 50% pay cut.

00:17:03.470 --> 00:17:04.303
No, thank you.

00:17:04.303 --> 00:17:05.150
A 100% paycut. No.

00:17:05.150 --> 00:17:07.330
Look, man, I'll do it for scale.

00:17:07.330 --> 00:17:09.070
No, we don't want you, you stay.

00:17:09.070 --> 00:17:11.380
You're the romcom guy stay over there.

00:17:11.380 --> 00:17:12.530
Oh damn it.

00:17:12.530 --> 00:17:14.500
Well, that coincides with the fact

00:17:14.500 --> 00:17:17.810
that I've now met the woman
who later became my wife,

00:17:17.810 --> 00:17:20.810
but I knew I was fell in love
with her very quickly, Camila.

00:17:21.987 --> 00:17:26.280
And we had our first child,

00:17:26.280 --> 00:17:27.660
she had the child, we
made the child, right?

00:17:27.660 --> 00:17:29.401
So I have a first child coming to town.

00:17:29.401 --> 00:17:30.234
If you know from the book,

00:17:30.234 --> 00:17:31.900
my life's dream was to be a dad, man.

00:17:31.900 --> 00:17:36.723
So I'm like all of a sudden,
my life is full and vital.

00:17:37.591 --> 00:17:40.510
I get madder,

00:17:40.510 --> 00:17:41.770
I get sadder,

00:17:41.770 --> 00:17:43.050
I laugh louder,

00:17:43.050 --> 00:17:45.959
I have more joy. I'm
talking about meaning.

00:17:45.959 --> 00:17:46.792
Okay.

00:17:46.792 --> 00:17:48.081
I got meaning in my life, right?

00:17:48.081 --> 00:17:48.914
Got you.

00:17:48.914 --> 00:17:52.355
My life is not bouncing cloud
the cloud, like a romcom.

00:17:52.355 --> 00:17:53.970
So I'm going, Hey, well,

00:17:53.970 --> 00:17:57.500
I wish my work could challenge
the vitality of my life,

00:17:57.500 --> 00:18:00.017
but it doesn't, oh damn it.

00:18:00.017 --> 00:18:02.580
So if I can't do what I want to do,

00:18:02.580 --> 00:18:05.100
I'm going to quit doing
what I've been doing.

00:18:05.100 --> 00:18:06.770
So I said no to romcoms,

00:18:06.770 --> 00:18:09.800
made the call to my money
man, to my agent and stuff.

00:18:09.800 --> 00:18:11.630
And I said, I'm not going
to do anymore romcoms,

00:18:11.630 --> 00:18:13.160
can you be a keeper at the gate

00:18:13.160 --> 00:18:15.060
and hold them back from coming my way.

00:18:16.440 --> 00:18:18.773
She had many a tear with Camila

00:18:18.773 --> 00:18:19.620
because this drought could be,

00:18:19.620 --> 00:18:21.630
I may not work, for I don't know how long.

00:18:21.630 --> 00:18:24.230
I may not ever work again in Hollywood.

00:18:24.230 --> 00:18:26.230
If I stick to this, which
when I was going to do it,

00:18:26.230 --> 00:18:28.096
I knew it was going to stick to it.

00:18:28.096 --> 00:18:31.030
So six months go by,

00:18:31.030 --> 00:18:33.500
I'm offered nothing but romcoms.

00:18:33.500 --> 00:18:34.333
No, thank you.

00:18:34.333 --> 00:18:35.166
No, thank you.

00:18:35.166 --> 00:18:35.999
No, thank you.

00:18:35.999 --> 00:18:36.832
No, thank you.

00:18:36.832 --> 00:18:37.950
I think a really fun
story just as I'm going

00:18:37.950 --> 00:18:39.080
on my preacher's box now,

00:18:39.080 --> 00:18:41.310
and I sound so puritanical.

00:18:41.310 --> 00:18:43.190
I think it was a really
nice story to tell you that.

00:18:43.190 --> 00:18:45.550
At month eight, without working,

00:18:45.550 --> 00:18:46.960
and there was a romcom that came in

00:18:46.960 --> 00:18:48.240
with a $5 million offer

00:18:48.240 --> 00:18:50.332
and I read it and I said, no.

00:18:50.332 --> 00:18:51.900
They came back with an $8 million offer.

00:18:51.900 --> 00:18:52.880
I said, no, thank you.

00:18:52.880 --> 00:18:54.940
They came back with a $12.5 million offer.

00:18:54.940 --> 00:18:55.773
I said, no, thank you.

00:18:55.773 --> 00:18:58.350
They came back with a $14.5 million offer.

00:18:58.350 --> 00:19:00.215
I said, let me read that thing again.

00:19:02.770 --> 00:19:04.640
How did it sound at that point?

00:19:04.640 --> 00:19:06.015
It was better.

00:19:06.914 --> 00:19:08.983
It was better, it was funnier.

00:19:08.983 --> 00:19:10.690
It was the exact same words
as the original offer,

00:19:10.690 --> 00:19:11.920
but it was a better script.

00:19:11.920 --> 00:19:12.891
Oh, yeah, it was.

00:19:12.891 --> 00:19:13.724
I get it.

00:19:13.724 --> 00:19:14.810
I ended up saying no.

00:19:14.810 --> 00:19:15.840
I'd said no to that.

00:19:15.840 --> 00:19:17.970
And that I believe in hindsight

00:19:17.970 --> 00:19:18.870
was a bit of a turning point.

00:19:18.870 --> 00:19:21.070
Meaning in Hollywood,

00:19:21.070 --> 00:19:23.340
that's when I think the studios

00:19:23.340 --> 00:19:25.440
that were had me on a
list to offer me movies.

00:19:25.440 --> 00:19:26.650
That's when they got the message, oh,

00:19:26.650 --> 00:19:28.050
McConaughey is not bluffing.

00:19:28.050 --> 00:19:29.050
Yeah, he's serious.

00:19:29.050 --> 00:19:30.610
He just turned it out for 14.5,

00:19:30.610 --> 00:19:31.781
and it's a good script,

00:19:31.781 --> 00:19:33.410
but he's not doing it.

00:19:33.410 --> 00:19:34.530
So I say no to that.

00:19:34.530 --> 00:19:38.154
Then nothing comes in for another year.

00:19:38.154 --> 00:19:40.474
Yeah, and you know what.

00:19:40.474 --> 00:19:42.540
And we know where it goes,

00:19:42.540 --> 00:19:43.730
and I do want to get into that,

00:19:43.730 --> 00:19:46.393
but like even pausing at this point, it's,

00:19:47.570 --> 00:19:49.303
I think we relate.

00:19:49.303 --> 00:19:51.600
Our situation is different,

00:19:51.600 --> 00:19:55.460
where we're in a different
world of entertainment,

00:19:55.460 --> 00:19:59.540
but we have these aspirations
of being filmmakers.

00:19:59.540 --> 00:20:01.963
And then with advent of YouTube.

00:20:03.285 --> 00:20:04.580
Yeah.

00:20:04.580 --> 00:20:06.800
That was our opportunity.

00:20:06.800 --> 00:20:07.633
Yeah.

00:20:07.633 --> 00:20:08.483
To develop our audience.

00:20:09.580 --> 00:20:12.670
We tried a lot of things before
landing on our daily show,

00:20:12.670 --> 00:20:14.060
Good Mythical Morning,

00:20:14.060 --> 00:20:15.850
which then became this thing

00:20:15.850 --> 00:20:18.440
that was a huge success.

00:20:18.440 --> 00:20:20.630
So now 10 years running,

00:20:20.630 --> 00:20:21.950
five days a week,

00:20:21.950 --> 00:20:23.540
we've been putting out

00:20:23.540 --> 00:20:26.240
a couple of videos a day for our show.

00:20:26.240 --> 00:20:28.512
Really connecting with our audience.

00:20:28.512 --> 00:20:30.960
It's super rewarding.

00:20:30.960 --> 00:20:34.750
So much of our career has come
from the opportunities that

00:20:34.750 --> 00:20:36.660
that show that we created

00:20:36.660 --> 00:20:40.750
and continue to evolve afforded us.

00:20:40.750 --> 00:20:41.727
Right.

00:20:41.727 --> 00:20:46.393
And now we're sitting
here 44, 43 years old,

00:20:47.530 --> 00:20:51.980
continuing to invest and
build a team around this show.

00:20:51.980 --> 00:20:52.860
Yeah.

00:20:52.860 --> 00:20:54.210
Also doing this podcast,

00:20:54.210 --> 00:20:57.680
which is a passion project of ours,

00:20:57.680 --> 00:21:00.260
which is doing really great too.

00:21:00.260 --> 00:21:02.970
But there's this itch, especially,

00:21:02.970 --> 00:21:05.523
I feel like at this point in our lives.

00:21:07.230 --> 00:21:08.120
I've got three kids,

00:21:08.120 --> 00:21:09.130
Rhett's got two kids,

00:21:09.130 --> 00:21:11.889
we've got happily married.

00:21:11.889 --> 00:21:15.020
We're living the dream

00:21:15.020 --> 00:21:20.020
that we shook with a blood
oath in middle school.

00:21:20.180 --> 00:21:23.563
And no matter what Rhett's
dad said or implied.

00:21:24.919 --> 00:21:27.392
we found a way to make it happen.

00:21:27.392 --> 00:21:28.225
Yeah.

00:21:28.225 --> 00:21:29.119
And now we're sitting here

00:21:29.119 --> 00:21:31.200
and we've got a lot of gas in the tank.

00:21:31.200 --> 00:21:32.110
Yeah.

00:21:32.110 --> 00:21:33.193
You built currency.

00:21:34.650 --> 00:21:36.471
So I feel like,

00:21:36.471 --> 00:21:38.540
I don't want to build
it up as a turning point

00:21:38.540 --> 00:21:41.341
or, but I do, maybe it is.

00:21:41.341 --> 00:21:42.174
Yeah.

00:21:42.174 --> 00:21:43.610
These are the things that
we're wrestling with.

00:21:43.610 --> 00:21:44.910
Well, we start asking the question,

00:21:44.910 --> 00:21:46.050
In terms of legacy.

00:21:46.050 --> 00:21:48.543
Is GMM our romcom, right?

00:21:48.543 --> 00:21:51.770
Because it's the thing that, it works.

00:21:51.770 --> 00:21:52.700
Yeah.

00:21:52.700 --> 00:21:54.435
There's great money in it.

00:21:54.435 --> 00:21:55.268
Right.

00:21:55.268 --> 00:21:56.101
It's the thing we're known for.

00:21:57.830 --> 00:22:00.740
You talk a little bit
about the fear, of sort of,

00:22:00.740 --> 00:22:05.053
you can make fear motivated decisions.

00:22:05.928 --> 00:22:06.761
Yeah.

00:22:06.761 --> 00:22:07.594
But you kind of had to
push that fear down.

00:22:07.594 --> 00:22:08.427
Well, you said early,

00:22:08.427 --> 00:22:09.260
You said good fear,

00:22:09.260 --> 00:22:10.093
the way you feared your dad,

00:22:10.093 --> 00:22:10.926
you had a good fear.

00:22:10.926 --> 00:22:14.810
Yeah, man. Well, one, congratulations.

00:22:14.810 --> 00:22:17.510
You guys are mid-40s, I'm 51.

00:22:17.510 --> 00:22:18.893
What do we call this?

00:22:21.417 --> 00:22:22.891
It's termed midlife crisis?

00:22:22.891 --> 00:22:23.724
Yeah.

00:22:23.724 --> 00:22:25.690
Well, whatever, at least
we're got the chutzpah

00:22:25.690 --> 00:22:27.800
to lean into some resistance.

00:22:27.800 --> 00:22:28.803
Yeah.

00:22:28.803 --> 00:22:29.636
Okay.

00:22:29.636 --> 00:22:30.469
And at least we got the chutzpah

00:22:30.469 --> 00:22:31.302
to question a blind spot,

00:22:31.302 --> 00:22:33.900
to question, oh, am I moving?

00:22:33.900 --> 00:22:34.883
I'm successful.

00:22:35.750 --> 00:22:38.840
And what I'm doing, is it significant?

00:22:38.840 --> 00:22:39.883
There's a different,

00:22:40.760 --> 00:22:45.760
Hey, also we don't want
to boohoo our romcoms.

00:22:45.800 --> 00:22:46.801
Right.

00:22:46.801 --> 00:22:47.634
You know what I mean?

00:22:47.634 --> 00:22:48.467
Definitely.

00:22:48.467 --> 00:22:52.329
Trust me. Remember, when I
was trying to make that choice

00:22:52.329 --> 00:22:53.300
and I was still doing the romcoms,

00:22:53.300 --> 00:22:55.790
I remember talking to my
great mentor, Penny Allen.

00:22:55.790 --> 00:22:57.043
She was like, what is bugging you about?

00:22:57.043 --> 00:23:01.230
I go, I just feel like
I'm just an entertainer.

00:23:01.230 --> 00:23:03.700
She goes, who the hell
do you think you are

00:23:03.700 --> 00:23:05.483
sloughing up being an entertainer?

00:23:06.513 --> 00:23:07.650
What do you got against
being just an entertainer.

00:23:07.650 --> 00:23:09.460
I was like, thank you.

00:23:09.460 --> 00:23:10.293
Put me in my place.

00:23:10.293 --> 00:23:12.144
So we have to give the credence,

00:23:12.144 --> 00:23:13.540
to what we've done

00:23:13.540 --> 00:23:15.740
and not try to make a
straight line crooked.

00:23:16.823 --> 00:23:17.656
Yeah.

00:23:17.656 --> 00:23:18.489
At the same time,

00:23:18.489 --> 00:23:20.100
we want to lean into, wait a minute, man,

00:23:20.100 --> 00:23:21.790
is there more here?

00:23:21.790 --> 00:23:23.250
And can it still be fun?

00:23:23.250 --> 00:23:24.083
Can we still have enjoy?

00:23:24.083 --> 00:23:25.424
Because it doesn't have to.

00:23:25.424 --> 00:23:27.316
I know what I have to watch is

00:23:27.316 --> 00:23:28.330
when things are going,

00:23:28.330 --> 00:23:30.220
I love to work hard,

00:23:30.220 --> 00:23:31.800
if my work is constructive.

00:23:31.800 --> 00:23:34.130
And I feel it working, I don't mind,

00:23:34.130 --> 00:23:35.850
if I can break a sweat
everyday, that's fun to me.

00:23:35.850 --> 00:23:37.050
I don't like to work hard

00:23:37.050 --> 00:23:38.050
and then be frustrated and be,

00:23:38.050 --> 00:23:39.670
I don't know where it went,

00:23:39.670 --> 00:23:40.503
but...

00:23:44.354 --> 00:23:47.140
I have to watch that if it's not hard,

00:23:47.140 --> 00:23:48.950
I don't think it's worthy.

00:23:48.950 --> 00:23:50.870
And sometimes my easiest stuff,

00:23:50.870 --> 00:23:52.660
when you sit there and you tell a joke

00:23:52.660 --> 00:23:53.810
and you malaprop the damn thing,

00:23:53.810 --> 00:23:54.950
and people laugh at the malaprop

00:23:54.950 --> 00:23:56.500
more than they did it at
the real joke, you're like,

00:23:56.500 --> 00:23:57.950
oh, geez, that was just an accident.

00:23:57.950 --> 00:23:58.790
That was the hit.

00:23:58.790 --> 00:23:59.840
Yeah.

00:23:59.840 --> 00:24:02.810
We'd go out and we go, I write the book.

00:24:02.810 --> 00:24:03.980
I got things in the book that I'm like,

00:24:03.980 --> 00:24:05.880
this is going to be the home run, man.

00:24:06.851 --> 00:24:09.390
Some of the stuff that
people never talk about that,

00:24:09.390 --> 00:24:12.060
it's this other stuff,
and I'm not going, oh.

00:24:12.060 --> 00:24:15.920
So I look for it sometimes
to be harder than it should

00:24:15.920 --> 00:24:17.240
and I have to balance that.

00:24:17.240 --> 00:24:19.420
So how do you lean into the blind spots,

00:24:19.420 --> 00:24:22.230
but also not just, like I said,

00:24:22.230 --> 00:24:24.120
don't not try to make a
straight line crooked,

00:24:24.120 --> 00:24:26.130
not trying to go, well,
it's not hard enough.

00:24:26.130 --> 00:24:27.987
This is worked out.

00:24:27.987 --> 00:24:29.450
We have to check in with ourselves.

00:24:29.450 --> 00:24:32.900
We come from small towns in the south.

00:24:32.900 --> 00:24:35.590
I dealt with that, kept my success early.

00:24:35.590 --> 00:24:37.410
Like, geez, why me?

00:24:37.410 --> 00:24:38.360
I deserve this.

00:24:38.360 --> 00:24:39.768
You know what I mean?

00:24:39.768 --> 00:24:42.010
Am I breaking a sweat enough?

00:24:42.010 --> 00:24:42.910
You know what I mean?

00:24:42.910 --> 00:24:46.610
So, congratulations on the questions guys.

00:24:46.610 --> 00:24:47.443
Yeah, and I think.

00:24:47.443 --> 00:24:48.909
I'm in them too in my life.

00:24:48.909 --> 00:24:51.700
Well, thanks for the congratulations.

00:24:51.700 --> 00:24:52.690
We appreciate that.

00:24:52.690 --> 00:24:56.730
And we are extremely
grateful for what we've got

00:24:56.730 --> 00:25:00.240
and the show is our friendship on display.

00:25:00.240 --> 00:25:03.000
And we can sit down behind our desk

00:25:03.000 --> 00:25:05.630
and we can just kind of be ourselves

00:25:05.630 --> 00:25:06.930
and people are drawn to it.

00:25:06.930 --> 00:25:08.830
And we've got great relationships

00:25:08.830 --> 00:25:09.877
with our fans because of it.

00:25:09.877 --> 00:25:13.030
And it allows us to launch other things

00:25:13.030 --> 00:25:15.920
that we want to do, to a certain level.

00:25:15.920 --> 00:25:17.520
But then you think about the stories

00:25:17.520 --> 00:25:18.410
that we want to tell

00:25:18.410 --> 00:25:22.290
and the filmmakers that we want to become.

00:25:22.290 --> 00:25:25.500
And there's a bit of a
chip on our shoulder.

00:25:25.500 --> 00:25:27.110
You got gratitude on one,

00:25:27.110 --> 00:25:28.739
and then I think there's
a chip on the other one.

00:25:28.739 --> 00:25:29.572
Sure. Yeah.

00:25:29.572 --> 00:25:31.700
It's like, okay, you
guys came up from YouTube

00:25:31.700 --> 00:25:34.793
and maybe there's a
little bit of parallel.

00:25:36.750 --> 00:25:40.660
When you sent the message to the business

00:25:40.660 --> 00:25:45.030
that you weren't even going to
take the $14.5 million offer.

00:25:45.030 --> 00:25:46.280
And that really did it.

00:25:46.280 --> 00:25:48.898
It's like, we're trying
to find a way to say,

00:25:48.898 --> 00:25:51.450
not to our fans, because they know,

00:25:51.450 --> 00:25:54.200
but to the gatekeepers to say, hey

00:25:54.200 --> 00:25:56.280
we're writing this thing and this thing,

00:25:56.280 --> 00:25:58.710
and we gotta get it into
the right person's hands,

00:25:58.710 --> 00:25:59.840
or we got to,

00:25:59.840 --> 00:26:03.240
we still have things we want
to say and assert ourselves.

00:26:03.240 --> 00:26:05.320
And it's different than
what we're known for

00:26:05.320 --> 00:26:06.910
and what we're successful for.

00:26:06.910 --> 00:26:07.743
Right.

00:26:12.568 --> 00:26:14.543
It's a bit frustrating.

00:26:15.488 --> 00:26:16.390
What's the frustrating part?

00:26:16.390 --> 00:26:17.807
Because you're not sure?

00:26:17.807 --> 00:26:19.769
You're not sure. Yeah, go ahead.

00:26:19.769 --> 00:26:23.160
Because you've already got,

00:26:23.160 --> 00:26:24.470
the fact that you're sitting here,

00:26:24.470 --> 00:26:26.010
we have microphones in front of us.

00:26:26.010 --> 00:26:27.309
I'm on your show.

00:26:27.309 --> 00:26:28.142
Yeah.

00:26:28.142 --> 00:26:30.330
You can be heard.

00:26:30.330 --> 00:26:31.510
Right.

00:26:31.510 --> 00:26:32.600
Even if you want to talk to,

00:26:32.600 --> 00:26:35.019
even if you're talking
to those people out there

00:26:35.019 --> 00:26:36.040
that may be able to say yes,

00:26:36.040 --> 00:26:36.993
let me see what you got, your script,

00:26:36.993 --> 00:26:38.733
I wanna maybe finance that.

00:26:39.709 --> 00:26:42.730
You already got a huge leg up.

00:26:42.730 --> 00:26:43.673
So you're in.

00:26:45.130 --> 00:26:45.963
Yeah.

00:26:49.400 --> 00:26:52.177
Two things came to mind when
you were talking there is,

00:26:52.177 --> 00:26:54.750
and this comes when we're not sure

00:26:54.750 --> 00:26:57.340
and being not sure is
obviously very healthy

00:26:57.340 --> 00:27:00.450
and true for us to be
maybe sometimes more.

00:27:00.450 --> 00:27:02.263
But when it's time to take action,

00:27:05.808 --> 00:27:08.820
y'all know you can't be
waiting for permission.

00:27:08.820 --> 00:27:09.791
Oh yeah.

00:27:09.791 --> 00:27:10.624
Yeah.

00:27:10.624 --> 00:27:12.150
Yeah. I know.

00:27:12.150 --> 00:27:14.829
You're gonna say a version of effort.

00:27:14.829 --> 00:27:15.767
Yeah.

00:27:15.767 --> 00:27:17.550
This is what we're doing
by hook or by crook,

00:27:17.550 --> 00:27:19.859
and we're not asking permission.

00:27:19.859 --> 00:27:22.193
And then it's going to be, probably,

00:27:23.511 --> 00:27:27.550
there's a line that I heard
somewhere along my line in life

00:27:27.550 --> 00:27:28.410
that I bring up in the book,

00:27:28.410 --> 00:27:30.010
that sometimes it's not about
which decision you make,

00:27:30.010 --> 00:27:32.317
it just about making damn
one and jumping and doing it

00:27:32.317 --> 00:27:33.520
and you find out.

00:27:33.520 --> 00:27:36.080
Yeah, should I get off
the pot, kind of thing.

00:27:36.080 --> 00:27:37.503
Go fucking find out. You know what I mean?

00:27:37.503 --> 00:27:38.336
Yeah. Right.

00:27:38.336 --> 00:27:39.169
Yeah.

00:27:39.169 --> 00:27:40.020
It's our relationship.

00:27:40.020 --> 00:27:41.120
For me, I know a lot of times

00:27:41.120 --> 00:27:43.340
it's my relationship with time.

00:27:43.340 --> 00:27:44.700
You get a decision like this,

00:27:44.700 --> 00:27:46.533
you start thinking, oh man,

00:27:46.533 --> 00:27:49.453
I don't know if I am I wasting
time, do I have enough time?

00:27:49.453 --> 00:27:50.286
Yeah.

00:27:50.286 --> 00:27:51.235
And all of a sudden you look up

00:27:51.235 --> 00:27:52.954
and you're 50 and you're going well,

00:27:52.954 --> 00:27:54.830
and you're then 55, then you're 60.

00:27:54.830 --> 00:27:55.663
Yeah.

00:27:55.663 --> 00:27:57.174
When we were 46, why didn't we just go.

00:27:58.570 --> 00:28:00.750
Let's see. And plus, and
then what did we find out?

00:28:00.750 --> 00:28:03.020
Oh, time is actually on our side.

00:28:03.020 --> 00:28:06.400
But if we head into it
without complacency,

00:28:06.400 --> 00:28:07.450
you know what I mean?

00:28:09.112 --> 00:28:12.080
It's another version of your story.

00:28:12.080 --> 00:28:14.940
Not that you're asking my advice on this,

00:28:14.940 --> 00:28:16.310
but you've got a show,

00:28:16.310 --> 00:28:17.900
are you pitching some of these stories?

00:28:17.900 --> 00:28:18.733
Yeah. Yeah.

00:28:18.733 --> 00:28:22.090
So I think the way we're
playing it right now is,

00:28:22.090 --> 00:28:25.120
really all along the way,
over the past 10 years,

00:28:25.120 --> 00:28:28.360
since we've been in LA, we've been,

00:28:28.360 --> 00:28:29.940
the business has been built around

00:28:29.940 --> 00:28:31.470
what we've been doing online

00:28:31.470 --> 00:28:33.210
and that's where we built
our team and our studio.

00:28:33.210 --> 00:28:34.790
And that's all working really well.

00:28:34.790 --> 00:28:37.610
In the meantime, we've been
like, we've been writing pilots,

00:28:37.610 --> 00:28:38.820
we've been writing features.

00:28:38.820 --> 00:28:42.830
We're doing that and we
are getting in the rooms

00:28:42.830 --> 00:28:44.843
and it's a completely different process.

00:28:45.780 --> 00:28:48.099
Over here on the internet, it's like idea

00:28:48.099 --> 00:28:50.330
put together the team, execute it,

00:28:50.330 --> 00:28:51.280
see what they think about it,

00:28:51.280 --> 00:28:52.310
see what the audience thinks about it.

00:28:52.310 --> 00:28:54.260
There's no gatekeeper when we get into

00:28:54.260 --> 00:28:55.750
the traditional Hollywood thing,

00:28:55.750 --> 00:28:58.910
we kind of deal with the
gatekeepers of it all.

00:28:58.910 --> 00:28:59.743
Yeah.

00:28:59.743 --> 00:29:01.273
So I think we're kind of in a,

00:29:01.273 --> 00:29:02.489
let's have our cake and eat it too.

00:29:02.489 --> 00:29:03.322
Sure.

00:29:03.322 --> 00:29:04.760
But when we both connected with that story

00:29:04.760 --> 00:29:06.930
when you were basically like, well,

00:29:06.930 --> 00:29:08.730
I'm gonna, and the dynamics are different,

00:29:08.730 --> 00:29:09.563
of course, but you were like,

00:29:09.563 --> 00:29:11.735
I'm gonna just turn the faucet off.

00:29:11.735 --> 00:29:12.568
Right, right.

00:29:12.568 --> 00:29:13.724
And they're going to be like,

00:29:13.724 --> 00:29:14.557
the only way you get this back on

00:29:14.557 --> 00:29:16.990
is if I'm doing what I
want to do specifically.

00:29:16.990 --> 00:29:17.944
Right.

00:29:17.944 --> 00:29:18.777
Right.

00:29:18.777 --> 00:29:19.630
And so that's kind of the thing,

00:29:19.630 --> 00:29:21.606
that's sort of ruminating right now.

00:29:21.606 --> 00:29:22.439
Yeah.

00:29:22.439 --> 00:29:24.816
Is like, what does that mean for us?

00:29:24.816 --> 00:29:25.649
Right.

00:29:25.649 --> 00:29:26.482
Because it's not like, hey,

00:29:26.482 --> 00:29:27.315
we're going to stop doing the show.

00:29:27.315 --> 00:29:28.148
That's the only way for us

00:29:28.148 --> 00:29:28.981
to move to the next thing.

00:29:28.981 --> 00:29:29.814
Right.

00:29:29.814 --> 00:29:31.440
It isn't as clean as that for us.

00:29:31.440 --> 00:29:33.800
But I think that the question,

00:29:33.800 --> 00:29:35.857
bringing it back to the idea of legacy

00:29:35.857 --> 00:29:37.130
and what you're known for.

00:29:37.130 --> 00:29:38.316
Yeah.

00:29:38.316 --> 00:29:39.380
I think that's a part of it for us.

00:29:39.380 --> 00:29:41.450
It's just like, okay, if
I'm walking the street,

00:29:41.450 --> 00:29:42.920
if somebody recognizes me,

00:29:42.920 --> 00:29:45.015
it's going to be like
nine times out of 10,

00:29:45.015 --> 00:29:46.230
it's going to be from
Good Mythical Morning.

00:29:46.230 --> 00:29:47.450
And it's going to be all the episode

00:29:47.450 --> 00:29:50.592
where you guys ate the pig testicles.

00:29:51.470 --> 00:29:52.620
Or whatever.

00:29:52.620 --> 00:29:54.390
And it's just like, okay, that's awesome,

00:29:54.390 --> 00:29:56.630
I'm glad that meant something to you.

00:29:56.630 --> 00:29:59.380
Do I want the thing that
nine out of 10 people

00:29:59.380 --> 00:30:00.300
will remember me for

00:30:00.300 --> 00:30:02.970
is eating pig testicles on the internet,

00:30:02.970 --> 00:30:03.940
or do I want to be like,

00:30:03.940 --> 00:30:06.120
hey, here's the thing that I wanna,

00:30:06.120 --> 00:30:07.820
there's a story that I wanna tell.

00:30:08.896 --> 00:30:09.729
Right.

00:30:09.729 --> 00:30:10.562
So what part of that
for you was like, okay.

00:30:10.562 --> 00:30:11.560
Heard.

